.TH TREE 1
.SH NAME
Tree \- an acme window's syntax tree, as an outline, in a rio window
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B Tree
[
.I winid
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tree
in the tag of an
.IR acme (1)
window opens a window of
.IR rio (1),
by
.B $wsys
.RI ( graphics (2)),
labeled
.IB file +Tree\f1,
that draws the parse of the acme window's text as an outline,
under a tag that looks and works as acme's
.RI ( adraw (2)).
The grammar is the one
.IR hl (1)
would color the file by, from the rules of
.B $home/lib/syn
or
.BR /lib/syn ,
and the parse is its program's answer to a
.B tree
request, which
.I Tree
asks of the one
.IR syn (4)
it runs.
Given
.IR winid ,
it shows that acme window instead of
.BR $winid .
.PP
Each row is a named node of the parse:
the name of its field in its parent, if it has one, its type, and some of its text:
a definition's name, a leaf's text, or, dimmer, the first line of a larger node's.
A triangle at the left of a node with children points down at an open node and right at a closed one.
At first the definitions that the language's
.B tags.scm
finds are open, with the nodes that hold them,
and the rest closed;
for a language without
.BR tags.scm ,
every node starts open.
.PP
.I Tree
follows the window by its
.B rdevent
file
.RI ( acme (4)).
It keeps the text, passes each insert and delete on to the grammar's program, as
.I hl
does,
and asks for a
.BR delta ,
so after an edit only what changed comes back, and the nodes still there stay open or closed as they were.
When
.B rdevent
leaves an insert's text out, as it does for a long one, or the program has lost the text,
.I Tree
reads the window's body and starts again.
.PP
.I Tree
highlights the row of the node at the window's selection.
When the selection moves into a closed node,
.I Tree
opens the nodes down to it, and closes them again when the selection leaves them;
a node opened or closed by hand stays so.
.PP
Button 1 on a triangle opens or closes its node.
Button 1 on a row selects its node and sets the window's selection to the node's text, and shows it.
The wheel scrolls.
.PP
Button 2 drags a row onto another, and the dragged node moves to just before that node,
or after it in the row's lower half, among its siblings.
Among the same siblings this reorders them, functions or statements or arguments,
and the text between siblings, its commas, blank lines and indentation, stays in place.
Onto another node, such as the
.B if
that holds a statement, the node leaves its siblings with the text that separated it,
and goes in with the text that separates its new siblings, indented as they are,
and with a semicolon if they end with one and it ends with neither one nor a brace
and does not parse there without it.
.I Tree
parses the result first.
It refuses a move that would make more errors than there were,
or change any node but the moved one, such as by adding an empty statement,
and says so.
When the window's text changes under a move or a pipe,
.I Tree
writes nothing and says the file changed.
.PP
.BI | cmd
typed in
.IR Tree 's
tag and run with button 2 runs
.I cmd
with
.IR rc (1)
in the file's directory, the selected node's text on its standard input,
and puts what it prints in the node's place in the window, in one write, as
.IR acme 's
.B |
does: a command that fails still has its output put there, and
.I Tree
says how it failed.
Undo takes it back, and text that no longer parses shows as error nodes.
The command runs while
.I Tree
goes on, and
.B Kill
in the tag stops it.
.I Tree
writes nothing if the text changed while the command ran or
.B Kill
stopped it.
One command runs at a time, and a
.B |
with no command runs nothing.
When the node's text has no newline at its end,
.I Tree
drops one the command adds there.
.PP
.B Undo
and
.B Redo
in
.IR Tree 's
tag, or control-Z and control-R typed there,
undo and redo the window it shows, as its own do.
Other words in the tag run as
.IR adraw (2)'s
tag runs them, with
.B $winid
and
.B $%
the window it shows.
.B Del
in the tag closes
.IR Tree 's
window and ends it, and leaves the window it shows;
so does rio's
.B Delete
of the window, and acme's
.B Kill
of
.IR Tree .
Deleting the window it shows ends
.I Tree
too and closes its window.
When that window takes a new name,
.I Tree
renames its tag and its label and takes the new name's grammar, or says there is none.
A grammar's program that answers
.B tree
with an error, as
.B /bin/ts/pic
does, gets no more requests, and
.I Tree
shows its answer.
.PP
Its colors are
.IR theme (6)'s
.B body
and
.BR bodysel ,
with
.B comment
for field names and the dim text, and
.B function
for definitions' names.
.SH EXAMPLES
In a C file's window, run
.BR Tree ,
select a function's row with button 1 in
.IR Tree 's
window, then type this in its tag and sweep it with button 2
.IP
.EX
|sed 's/count/n/g'
.EE
.PP
to rename a variable in that function alone.
.SH FILES
.TF /sys/lib/ts/lang/tags.scm
.TP
.B /lib/syn
the languages' rules
.TP
.BI /sys/lib/ts/ lang /tags.scm
what a language's definitions are
.TP
.BI /bin/ts/ lang
a language's program
.PD
.SH SOURCE
plan9web's
.B src/cmd/Tree.c
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR acme (1),
.IR hl (1),
.IR rio (1),
.IR adraw (2),
.IR graphics (2),
.IR acme (4),
.IR syn (4),
.IR theme (6)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Tree
needs a window system:
without
.B $wsys
it says so and exits.
.SH BUGS
A move works on text alone.
A node that leaves an expression takes the operator before or after it along,
and nothing takes its place: hoisting does not make a variable for it.
After each edit
.B tags
runs over the whole text.
.PP
Acme's
.B seq
guard is one for each window, taken by the next open:
.I hl
or another program arming it between
.IR Tree 's
write of
.B seq
and its open can make that open fail, or take the guard
.I Tree
set.
.PP
.I Tree
writes a change of the window's text in pieces that end where a rune does, as one Undo;
an edit by another program between two pieces leaves part of the change written.
